  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "The Boreal Bolete is a small to medium-sized mushroom characterized by its sticky, glandular cap and yellow pores underneath. It belongs to the Suillaceae family and forms mycorrhizal relationships with coniferous trees, particularly larches and pines.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Sticky, Glandular Cap",
      "description": "The cap has a distinctive viscid (sticky) surface covered with small glandular dots, especially when moist, which is the origin of its species name 'glandulosus'.",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Yellow Pores",
      "description": "The undersurface features bright yellow pores that may become darker or stain brownish when bruised or with age.",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Partial Veil Remnants",
      "description": "Young specimens often display a partial veil connecting the cap edge to the stem, which leaves a ring-like zone on the upper stem.",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Found in New England's boreal and mixed coniferous forests, typically fruiting in summer and fall near larch (tamarack), pine, and occasionally spruce trees in moist to boggy areas.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "The glandular dots on the Boreal Bolete's cap are specialized structures that actively secrete droplets, making the mushroom literally 'weep' in humid conditions—a trait shared by few other boletes.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
  "contextualKnowledge": null,
